I think there is a way to handle this that preserves the success of last Monday's presentation, demonstrates appropriate follow-through, and substantially reduces the risk of getting pulled back into another open-ended Blue Sky analysis cycle.

My recommendation is not to send the new PowerPoint, not to immediately schedule another meeting with Billy or Brandon, but also not to simply let the matter disappear.

Instead, I recommend a deliberately limited follow-up: review the new results, determine whether they materially change the conclusions you presented last Monday, and then decide whether a brief communication is warranted. I would lean toward a short, informal conversation with Jordan only if you need help interpreting a specific finding—not as an invitation to brainstorm additional analyses.

There is an important distinction here: You don't need to produce another impressive analysis to finish this project successfully. You need to determine whether the questions raised last Monday change the decision.

I also think you're underestimating the significance of what happened last Monday. Let's examine that first, because it should influence your decision.

1. Last Monday was a meaningful success

From what you've described, the November 2 presentation was substantially better than the preceding weeks of Blue Sky work.

You had spent a long time producing simulations, plots, and revisions of the presentation. You'd struggled to identify the central story and questioned whether the analysis 
was useful. Yet when you finally presented to Billy, Brandon, Jordan, and Max:

- You delivered a simple, understandable message.
- Billy understood your central conclusion.
- You reinforced that conclusion during the discussion.
- You felt confident and clear while speaking.
- The group engaged with the analysis and asked substantive questions.

This is especially important because one of the recurring challenges you've described is difficulty speaking confidently in group settings. Here, you managed to do precisely what you've been working toward.

I wouldn't automatically interpret the additional questions as a sign that the presentation was incomplete or insufficient. Technical and commercial leaders often ask questions even when a presentation successfully 
answers the central business question.

And Billy's closing comment—something along the lines of finding something interesting and getting together for a huddle—sounds more like an invitation to continue exploring than an explicit assignment with a deadline.

Of course, there's ambiguity. He might reasonably expect some follow-through, particularly because you said you'd look at 
the additional scenarios.

But the absence of a formal assignment and the presence of a successful presentation give you considerably more discretion over what happens next.

There is also a psychological asymmetry worth recognizing. You seem to assign enormous importance to the possibility that a mediocre follow-up will undermine the good presentation, while 
simultaneously worrying that an absence of follow-up will be interpreted as poor performance.

Both risks are plausible. Neither is inevitable. The objective should be to manage both risks with as little additional work as reasonably possible.

2. Comparing your options

I see six meaningful options, including two that fall between your proposed alternatives.

Option A. Do nothing

Benefits: Preserves last Monday's successful ending; frees up time; avoids reopening analysis.

Risks: Questions remain unanswered; could appear that you didn't follow through.

My assessment: Tempting, but unnecessarily risky.

Option B. Review results, then 
stop

Benefits: You gain clarity without involving anyone; preserves your time.

Risks: No visible follow-through, even if results are useful.

My assessment: Good first step, but not necessarily the endpoint.

Option C. Send the new PowerPoint to everyone

Benefits: Demonstrates activity; 
creates a record; distributes findings.

Risks: May muddy the original message, expose unclear work, and invite more requests.

My assessment: I would avoid this.

Option D. Send a short, focused email

Benefits: Demonstrates responsiveness; can explicitly reinforce the original 
conclusion; limits the scope.

Risks: Could generate additional questions; requires a defensible conclusion.

