6. What if you simply don't follow up?

I want to give that option serious consideration, rather than treating it as inherently unprofessional.

Your circumstances matter.

You've already received your 2026 performance ranking, and you're transitioning out 
of the group. You're facing other unfinished deliverables, and you have good reasons to believe that continuing Blue Sky analysis could consume disproportionate amounts of your remaining time.

You're also concerned about Alex's evaluation of your performance and the impressions Billy and Brandon have formed.

But here's a distinction I'd make: It may be too late 
to fundamentally change someone's established assessment of your work, but it isn't too late to demonstrate a competent, orderly handover.

If you were to spend the next three weeks trying to produce the definitive Blue Sky analysis, there's no guarantee that anyone would change their opinion of you. You could consume your final weeks on an inherently open-ended project, at the expense of closing out other valuable work.

Conversely, spending an hour reviewing results, sending a concise summary, and archiving the underlying analysis could accomplish most of the reputational benefit at a fraction of the cost.

I would also distinguish between two forms of silence:

- Unintentional silence: You avoid the results because you fear they won't be good enough, and the question remains 
unresolved in your mind.
- Deliberate closure: You review the evidence, determine that no further work is justified, and document the status appropriately.

The second can be a rational professional decision, even if you choose not to send a broad email.

The first is more likely to leave you worrying about Blue Sky for the remainder of November.

7. One boundary I would establish

I would avoid using "interesting" as your criterion for whether to pursue additional work.

Billy may have used that word casually. It doesn't necessarily mean he expects you to independently discover another compelling story before you leave the group.

Technical analysis can always produce another interesting 
comparison, another distribution, another sensitivity, and another question.

You need a stopping rule.

I would use this:

Further analysis is justified only if it could materially change the Blue Sky decision, resolve an explicitly important uncertainty, or complete a specifically assigned deliverable.

The fact that something could 
be analyzed is not sufficient justification to analyze it.

If someone requests another study, you can ask which decision it will inform and whether it should take priority over your remaining transition work. That's a legitimate question, particularly as your transfer approaches.

